  "prompt_text": "The figure links local biology inside a knee joint with body-wide neural pathways. On the left, a cross-section of the joint shows synovial fluid between the femur and tibia, bordered by articular cartilage and the synovium, with nearby capsule and infrapatellar fat pad. The main message is that synovial fluid is not just a lubricant: it contains signaling molecules and RNA species, including metabolites, proteins, cytokines, growth factors, miRNAs, circRNAs, and lncRNAs. Arrows indicate two-way exchange between the fluid and surrounding tissues, emphasizing inter-tissue communication across the synovium and cartilage and likely between multiple joint compartments.\n\nOn the right, a human outline highlights the nervous and circulatory systems, showing how signals from peripheral tissues can connect to dorsal root ganglia, the spinal cord, and ultimately the brain. A boxed region in the leg points to dorsal root neuron projections, suggesting that signals originating in the joint may be transmitted through sensory neurons while also interacting with blood vessels. Taken together, the image presents the joint as part of a broader communication network, where molecules in synovial fluid may influence local tissue behavior and potentially connect to systemic and neural responses.",
